// Industrial pressure-sensor node — board layer.
//
// The SECOND example system's board (Phase 3 §13). It exists to prove the
// engine is generic, so it is deliberately different from the scooter's boards
// in the ways that would catch something hardcoded:
//
//     CAN   fdcan2, not fdcan1
//     UART  usart2, not usart3
//     bus   250 kbit/s, not 500
//
// If any of those names or numbers were baked into the harness rather than read
// out of the board table and the topology, this board is what fails.
//
// Same chip layer as the scooter's boards, because the point being tested is
// the engine's independence from a board, not Renode's coverage of a second
// silicon vendor. A different vendor is a board-library question and belongs in
// Phase 5, where the tier system already says what it would cost.
using "../nucleo_h743zi_can.repl"

// Nothing else. Sensor values are injected at variable level (PROJECT.md §6),
// so there is no transducer to model, and a board file that invented one would
// be a placeholder pretending to be a specification.
//
// fdcan2 and usart2 are already present in the chip layer:
//     fdcan2: CAN.MCAN @ sysbus 0x4000A400, messageRAM: canMessageRAM
//     usart2: UART.STM32F7_USART @ sysbus 0x40004400
//
// fdcan1 and fdcan2 share canMessageRAM in Renode's STM32H743 description, as
// they do on the real part. This node uses only fdcan2, and each node runs in
// its own emulated machine, so nothing else is allocating out of that region.
